#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Companion OS: one-click automatic install, from the live session.

The user clicks "Install Companion OS (automatic)". This tool picks the internal disk, shows ONE
confirmation that names the disk and what is on it, and then drives Canonical's own installer
(ubuntu-desktop-bootstrap's subiquity server) through its autoinstall path with no further questions:
English, US keyboard, time zone from geoip, the Default application selection, "Erase disk and install".
The confirmation has one more button, "Language, keyboard, time zone...", that changes those first three.

It does not replace or modify the installer. It writes an autoinstall file, restarts the installer's
server so that it loads it, confirms the install over the server's own API and reports progress.

Safety rules (each one is tested in backends/ubuntu/chroot/tests/test_auto_install.py):
  * Never the boot medium. Every disk that carries /cdrom, /isodevice or a Companion Stick partition
    (CI-EFI, CI-SHARED, CI-BOOT-A/B, CI-CACHE, CI-PERSIST, writable, casper-rw) is excluded. A /cdrom on a loop
    device is followed to the disk that holds the ISO file. If the boot medium cannot be identified the tool
    REFUSES (fail closed) and offers the normal installer instead.
  * Only internal disks: not removable, not hotplug, not USB/Thunderbolt-attached, not an SD/eMMC card, not
    read-only, big enough (25 GB). With more than one such disk the user chooses; nothing is guessed.
  * One confirmation that names model, size, device and everything found on the disk. Enter never confirms:
    the dialog's default is Cancel.
  * The target is pinned by an early-command inside the install itself: if the disk's size is not the
    one that was shown, or the disk now carries /cdrom, the install aborts before it writes anything. The
    disk list is also re-checked after the click and again just before the install is confirmed.
  * One instance at a time (flock), and it refuses to touch an installer that is already installing.
  * The sign-in password is random, shown in the confirmation and in the progress window, and must be changed
    at first login. Only its hash goes into the autoinstall file, which lives on tmpfs and is removed
    afterwards. A foreign /autoinstall.yaml is never overwritten or removed.

Usage:
  ci-os-auto-install                 interactive (zenity), the normal entry point
  ci-os-auto-install --plan          print the chosen disk and the autoinstall file (password hash masked),
                                     change nothing. Exit 0, or 3 when no safe disk exists
  --locale de_DE.UTF-8 --keyboard de --timezone Europe/Berlin
                                     the starting answers of the dialog (and of --plan); default en_US.UTF-8, us, geoip
  ci-os-auto-install --disks         print the candidate/excluded disk table
  ci-os-auto-install --live-setup    desktop-session autostart hook: in a live session only, put the launcher on the
                                     desktop, in the app grid and first in the dock (the 26.04 live session draws no
                                     desktop icons); with ci.autoinstall=1 on the kernel command line
                                     (the boot menu's automatic entry) it also starts the install dialog
Environment (tests only): CI_OS_AI_LSBLK_JSON, CI_OS_AI_CDROM_SOURCE, CI_OS_AI_LOOP_BACKING ("<device> <fstype>"),
CI_OS_AI_CMDLINE read fixtures instead of the system.

Exit codes: 0 ok, 2 usage, 3 no safe target / medium unknown, 4 not a live session, 5 installer not ready or busy,
6 user cancelled (or another instance is open), 7 install failed.
"""
